WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.775
Local operation can improve privacy, but local does not automatically mean secure.

00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:07.400
Model files, runtime packages, extensions,

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.700
and installers are software supply-chain inputs.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.770
Obtain them from reputable sources, verify hashes or signatures when available,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.700
review license and provenance information,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.500
and avoid executing unfamiliar model packages that require custom code.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.540
Keep an inventory so an affected component can be identified when a security advisory

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.200
appears.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.779
Treat a local AI API like any other service.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:30.895
Bind it to the loopback interface unless another system genuinely needs access.

00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:35.600
If network access is required, use host firewall rules, authentication, encryption,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.900
and a limited trusted network.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.700
Do not expose an unauthenticated inference endpoint directly to the internet.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.640
An attacker could consume compute resources, access connected data sources,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.370
or use the service as an entry point into the workstation.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.340
Separate users and workloads where practical.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.500
Protect sensitive prompts, retrieved documents, chat histories, API keys,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.755
and logs with appropriate file permissions and storage controls.

00:00:56.755 --> 00:01:00.230
Understand which applications retain conversations and whether telemetry leaves the

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:00.790
machine.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.230
A model can reproduce sensitive material supplied in its context,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.400
so local processing does not remove the need for data handling rules.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.128
Reliability is part of security.

00:01:09.128 --> 00:01:11.600
Back up configuration files and important data,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.295
but avoid backing up huge model collections blindly when they can be re-downloaded.

00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:18.695
Record known-good versions and export settings before major changes.

00:01:18.695 --> 00:01:22.695
Apply operating system, driver, runtime, and interface patches deliberately,

00:01:22.695 --> 00:01:24.275
then test a representative workflow.

00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:29.130
A stable configuration snapshot and a rollback plan allow experimentation without turning

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.940
every update into a recovery exercise.
